<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bengal Subah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a>–<a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Mughal Bengal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subah">Subah</a> was the province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> covering Bengal, roughly present-day Bangladesh and the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, at times with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. Conquered from the Karrani sultans in 1576, it became one of the empire's richest provinces, known for textiles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. Its governors fought repeated wars with the Ahom kingdom, and from 1717 the Nawabs of Bengal ruled it with growing independence until the British victory at Plassey in 1757.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bengal-subah.jpg" alt="Bengal Subah" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greatswrd · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengal_Subah_Standard.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
